WHY THIS WORKS
- explains the program without reciting nutrition, sleep, stress as a checklist
- states plainly what it is not, which is what she is really asking
- arc 2 accepts that the wrong fit is not a failure and does not argue

CONVERSATION 1: asking what she would actually be buying

Lead: I keep seeing your posts. Can you explain what your program actually is? I've been burned by fertility programs before.

Sonia: Reasonable to ask, and reasonable to be wary.

I'm a fertility coach, not a doctor or a clinic. I don't perform IVF and I don't prescribe. What I do is work out which handful of things actually matter in your case, put them in an order, and then stay with you while you do them.

Lead: everyone says something like that though. what does it mean in practice?

Sonia: Fair. In practice it means I go through your history, your cycles, your partner's side, how you're eating and sleeping and recovering, and what's already been tried, and then I tell you which 2 or 3 things are worth your energy and which ones aren't.

Then the part most programs skip: I stay with you while you change them, and notice when you stop. Information isn't what most people are missing.

Lead: How is that different from what I got last time, which was a PDF and a Facebook group?

Sonia: It's a coaching relationship, not a product. There's a plan built for your case, and there's someone on the other end of it who notices when you stop doing it.

What went wrong with the last one?

Lead: I did it for three weeks, got busy and there was nobody to notice.

Sonia: Then that's the exact gap. A plan you don't run isn't a plan, and nobody was accountable for whether you ran it.

Lead: OK. What's the investment?

Sonia: {{price_range}}, depending on the level of support. Different people need very different amounts of it.

Lead: And I'd need to decide before a call?

Sonia: No. The call is free and its whole purpose is working out whether I can actually help you. If I can't, they'll tell you.

Lead: That's what I wanted to hear.

Sonia: Then book whatever time works.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 2: she wants a course, not coaching

Lead: Do you have a self-paced course? I don't really want calls or check-ins, I just want the information.

Sonia: I don't, no. What I offer is coaching, and the check-ins are the part that makes it work, not an add-on.

Lead: I'm quite disciplined though, I'd rather just have the material.

Sonia: I believe you, and I'd still be selling you the wrong thing.

Most of what I do is bio-individual. The same lab result means different things in two different women, so a set of materials without someone reading your case is guesswork with better production values.

Lead: So there's nothing for me?

Sonia: Not from me at that level, and I'd rather say so than take your money.

My masterclass is free and it's substantial. It'll give you the framework and better questions for whoever you work with next.

{{masterclass_link}}

Lead: Appreciate the honesty.

Sonia: If you ever want the other version of this, you know where I am.

CONVERSATION 3: she asks twice for a detail nobody has given me

Lead: how many months does the program run for?

Sonia: The length depends on the level of support, and my team set that on the call rather than me guessing at it here.

Lead: roughly though? like 3 months or 6?

Sonia: I'm not going to put a number on it just because you asked twice. If I say six and yours is three, you'll have heard six from me and it'll be the thing you remember.

What I can tell you is what decides it: how much is already in place, what your treatment timeline looks like, and how much support you actually want. That's the conversation the call is for, and it's a short one.

Lead: fine. but you must have some idea

Sonia: I have an idea. It isn't reliable enough to hand you as a fact about your case, and a guess with my name on it is worse than no answer.

Ask them on the call, first question, and hold them to a straight answer.

Lead: ok, that's fair enough

Sonia: What's making the timing matter? If something's coming up, that's more useful to talk about than the length.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

